The deep dive — the actual answer. An S cubic hits the integer lattice in exactly three places at scale 1: t = 0, ½, 1, i.e. (0,0), (c,0), (2c,0). There are no others, at any rational t. The reason is arithmetic: at t = k/q the coordinates have denominator q³, so a fraction only becomes integer when the drawing is scaled by q³.
scale	q	S hits	C hits
1	1	3	2–3
8	2	3	3
27	3	5	4–5
64	4	5	5
125	5	9	6–9
1000	10	11	11
15625	25	29	26–29
So "key distances" are q³, not 625: 625 and 3125 do not add hits to a cubic (3125 is the quintic's 5⁵). That table is now a section on integer-cubic-catalogue.html, computed by lattice_hits() / hit_table() in curve_lab/pyth_cubic.py.
