Curves with κ ∝ sᵖ are the generalised Euler spirals (Levien's family): p = 1 is the clothoid, p = 0 a circle, p = −1 the logarithmic spiral. This one sits at about p = −1.24, so it's a member of that family, not a new species.
And there's a prediction hiding in it. For large m, θ ≈ 2n/m and the arc length grows like m³, so κ ≈ dθ/ds ∝ s^-4/3. The measured −1.24 should therefore drift toward −1.333 as the band extends.

What the two things mean. C1 means the curve doesn't kink at a join — it leaves a node in the same direction it arrived. G2 means the bend doesn't kink either — the tightness matches across the join. C1 is about direction; G2 is about curvature.
Why C1 is free. Both spans on either side of a node use the same tangent vector, so the direction matches automatically. Nothing to solve, no variables to set.
Why G2 isn't. With one handle length for the whole chain, the tightness at the end of one span and the start of the next don't agree — they're 6% apart typically, 26% at worst. You can't fix that by choosing the handle length, because one number can't satisfy many joins.
What the per-node button does. It gives each node its own handle length. Now each join has a free number, and the equation Cₖ(S−hₖ) = Cₖ₊₁(S−hₖ₊₂) is just the statement "make the two sides bend by the same amount." Solve it and the curvature difference at every join becomes exactly zero.
So yes, you set two variables: the first two handle lengths (h₀, h₁). Everything after that is calculated from them by that rule. Two dials, then the chain is fully determined.
The catch. The answers are fractions — 1, 1, 29/23, 37/30, …. Fractions are fine for a drawing, but they're not integers. To make the control points whole numbers again you'd multiply everything by the common denominator, and that number has 84 digits. The grid would be unusable.
So the trade is: whole-number control points with a small curvature kink (C1), or perfect smoothness with fractional control points (G2). Not both, from this construction.
Why you might not care. A 6% curvature jump is invisible as a line. It stops being invisible when you give the curve a width — offsets amplify curvature discontinuities into visible flat spots or pinches. So for a spine, C1 is fine. For a stem with rails, G2 would earn its keep.
Three ways out, if you want G2 on integers:
1. Accept fractions and a scaled grid — exact, impractical.
2. Use a different construction: the mirror pairs in pyth_g2.py give exact G2 with integers, but only for symmetric pairs.
3. Search small chains for m ranges where the recurrence happens to produce whole numbers. Nobody has tried this; it's cheap to test and would be a genuinely new result if a family exists.
Option 3 is the interesting one, and it's a one-function experiment. Want me to run it?

