I'm here. My honest ranking, not a summary of everything that ran.
1. The negative results — the biggest success.
We now know, with numbers, that: a constant-width parallel of a log spiral is not a log spiral (drift 0.21°→2.6° with d/r); a parallel of a parabola is not a parabola (21% of d); three different Euclid families cannot run parallel (gap 0 → 2,572 over twelve steps); and the node chords of the integer chain are not Pythagorean, so no polygon through them offsets exactly. Each of those could have cost a day of hopeful work. They are recorded as refusals, with the arithmetic that justifies them.
2. The naming, because it forced the thinking.
"Turn-exact" versus "Euclid family chains" — names that state what each keeps exact instead of what role it plays — turned a muddle into a real distinction: exact turns with c^k denominators, versus integer ink at scale 1. That is the kind of clarity that changes what gets built next.
3. One genuinely new building block.
curve_lab/taper.py: the exact partner is scaled and rotated, width ∝ radius. It is the only exact variable-width stem in the catalogue, and it ships with its own defect stated (5.25% asymmetry). A tool with a known tolerance is worth more than a pretty curve.
4. The parabola result.
Your hypothesis was testable, and the answer was better than yes or no: the involute of the evolute reproduces the parabola to 1e-15 and gives the parallels exactly — which simultaneously proves they are not parabolas. We resolved the question rather than refuting it.
5. The method, which is the real success.
- Measuring caught two of my own errors: curvature_at dividing by |u|⁶, and the log-spiral width using the far intersection (1.60·r) instead of the nearest (0.3409·r). Both were published before they were checked; both are now tested.
- The ledger went from 14 to 20 checks, including the negative ones, so a fresh session starts from the evidence rather than from the last session's confidence.
- Tests guard claims, not files — and the TODO twin test caught me editing TODO.md without regenerating its page.
What I would not call a success yet: the plant-form grammar is still not proved end to end; there is no exact constant-width stem; and the integer chain's advantage remains narrow — small exact numbers and exact nodes, with a width that is still floating point. Whether that is enough to keep is exactly the question Fig. E1 and Fig. Y were built to answer.
If I had to name the single best thing: the project has become good at proving its own dead ends cheaply. That is what makes the successes above trustworthy.
